🧠 1. Choice of System (ECU-style engine control)
Decision:

You modelled an internal combustion engine with an electronic control unit regulating air-fuel ratio and engine speed.

Why this matters:
It is a classic feedback control problem
It has real industrial relevance (automotive ECU design)
It naturally includes:
nonlinear dynamics
disturbances
constraints
Trade-off:
You simplify real engine physics heavily
But gain a tractable, analysable control system
🧩 2. Separation into Engine, Controller, and Scenario
Decision:

You split the system into:

Engine model (physics)
Controller (C++)
Scenario + simulation (Python)
Configuration (YAML)
Why:

This mimics real engineering systems:

plant ≠ controller ≠ test conditions
Benefit:
modular design
easy swapping of controllers (PID vs LQR)
clean experiments
Trade-off:
more architecture work
but significantly better evaluation capability
⚙️ 3. State-Space Choice (what variables exist)
Decision:

You defined engine state as:

air mass
fuel mass
engine speed

and derived:

air-fuel ratio
Why:
minimal set that still produces meaningful dynamics
supports feedback control naturally
Trade-off:
not physically exact
but structurally realistic
🔁 4. Feedback Control Design
Decision:

Controller does NOT compute physics — it only:

compares target vs measured air-fuel ratio
outputs fuel injection
Why:

This is the core principle of control systems:

separate “what we want” from “how we achieve it”

Trade-off:
requires a separate target model
but improves clarity and realism
🎯 5. Target Air-Fuel Ratio is Scenario-Based
Decision:

Target air-fuel ratio is generated from:

throttle position
optionally engine speed
Why:

This mimics real ECU “maps”:

power demand → richer mixture
efficiency → lean mixture
Trade-off:
introduces extra model layer
but makes control problem meaningful (not trivial constant tracking)
📡 6. Sensor Noise and Imperfection
Decision:

Measured air-fuel ratio includes noise.

Why:

Real systems never observe true state.

Benefit:
tests robustness of controller
prevents “perfect simulation bias”
Trade-off:
adds instability complexity
requires tuning controller carefully
🧮 7. Continuous-Time Approximation (discrete timestep model)
Decision:

System evolves in discrete timesteps:

small Δt updates instead of continuous equations
Why:
computationally simple
easy to implement and debug
standard in simulation systems
Trade-off:
approximation of real physics
accuracy depends on timestep size
🔥 8. Engine Torque Model is Simplified
Decision:

Torque depends mainly on:

fuel mass
combustion efficiency
load resistance
Why:
keeps system nonlinear but manageable
ensures fuel has direct performance impact
Trade-off:
ignores full thermodynamics
but preserves control-relevant behaviour
⚖️ 9. Load is Linear in Engine Speed
Decision:

Load torque increases with engine speed.

Why:
represents friction + resistance in a simple form
ensures stable equilibrium exists
Trade-off:
real engines are more complex (nonlinear load curves)
but linear form keeps system analytically stable
🎛️ 10. PID Controller in C++
Decision:

Controller implemented using:

proportional term
integral term
derivative term
Why:
industry-standard baseline controller
easy to analyse and compare
Trade-off:
not optimal control (like MPC/LQR could be)
but excellent for demonstration and tuning
🧾 11. YAML-Based Configuration System
Decision:

All parameters stored externally:

engine constants
controller gains
scenarios
Why:
decouples code from experiments
enables repeatability
supports parameter sweeps
Trade-off:
extra parsing layer
but massively improves experiment design
🧪 12. Experiment-Driven Design
Decision:

System is designed around running experiments, not just simulation.

Why:

Your goal is evaluation:

step response
disturbance rejection
noise robustness
Benefit:
transforms project into “control system study”, not just code
📊 13. Offline Analysis Model
Decision:

Simulation logs data → analysis happens after run

Why:
allows reproducibility
enables comparison across controllers
avoids coupling analysis to runtime
Trade-off:
not real-time feedback
but much better scientific workflow
🧠 14. Key Abstraction Decision (most important)
Decision:

You explicitly separated:

Plant (engine) → evolves physical state
Controller (C++) → decides input
Observer (sensor) → imperfect measurement
Reference model (target AFR) → desired behaviour
Why this matters:

This is the core structure of all control systems.

🧭 Final System Philosophy

Your project is built on this principle:

“A nonlinear dynamic system is controlled via feedback using noisy measurements, with configurable parameters enabling systematic experimentation.”

